<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
==Description==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Inserts a subtractive wedge in the active Body. Its shape is subtracted from the existing solid.
</div>

[[File:PartDesign_SubtractiveWedge_example.svg|none]]
<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
''On the left: active body (A) shown in grey and subtractive wedge (B) shown in transparent red; result on the right.''
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
==Usage==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
# There are several ways to invoke the tool:
#* Press the {{Button|[[Image:PartDesign_SubtractiveWedge.svg|16px]] [[PartDesign_SubtractiveWedge|Subtractive Wedge]]}} button. '''Note''': the Subtractive Wedge is part of an icon menu labelled ''Subtractive Primitive''. After launching FreeCAD, the Subtractive Box is the one displayed in the toolbar. To get the Wedge, click on the down arrow besides the visible icon and select Subtractive Wedge in the menu.
#* Select the {{MenuCommand|Part Design → Subtractive Primitive → [[Image:PartDesign_SubtractiveWedge.svg|16px]] Subtractive Wedge}} option from the menu.
# Set the Primitive Parameters and [[Part_EditAttachment|Attachment]].
# Click {{Button|OK}}.
# A Wedge feature appears under the active Body.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
==Options==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
The Wedge can be edited after its creation in two ways:
* Double-clicking it in the [[Tree_View|Tree View]], or by right-clicking and selecting '''Edit Primitive''' in the context menu; this brings up the Primitive Parameters.
* Via the [[Property_View|Property View]].
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
=== Operation ===
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Sets the boolean operation used to combine the wedge with the base solid. {{Version|26.3}}
* '''Subtraction''' (default): the wedge removes its volume from the base solid, as in previous versions.
* '''Common''': the wedge keeps only the volume shared between itself and the base solid, discarding everything else.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
==Properties==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Using the default placement, the below inputs are:
*{{PropertyData|X min/max}} : Base face X-axis span
*{{PropertyData|Y min/max}}: Wedge height span
*{{PropertyData|Z min/max}} : Base face Z-axis span
*{{PropertyData|X2 min/max}} : Top face X-axis span
*{{PropertyData|Z2 min/max}} : Top face Z-axis span
* {{PropertyData|Operation|Enumeration}}: sets the boolean operation used to combine the feature with the base solid (Subtraction, Common), see [[#Options|Options]]. {{Version|26.3}}
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
==Pyramids==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Wedges can be used to create pyramids by setting {{PropertyData|X2 min/max}} and {{PropertyData|Z2 min/max}} each so that min = max.
</div>


